    thinking: "**Considering Mobile Responsiveness**\n\nOkay, I\'m focusing on making the application mobile responsive, starting with `App.tsx`. I\'m starting by examining its structure, specifically the layout, to identify the key areas to optimize. I\'m prioritizing elements in the top row.\n\n\n**Refining Mobile Layout**\n\nI\'m now refining the mobile layout within `App.tsx`. The current absolute positioning for the middle-right table is problematic; it breaks on small screens. I\'m considering a stacked grid or flex layout for mobile and desktop, or a multi-column desktop layout and a single-column on mobile. Also, the `h-screen` setting might not be optimal for mobile due to content height.\n\n\n**Optimizing Container and Row**\n\nI\'ve updated the overall container to use `min-h-screen`, and then made the top row more robust using `flex-col-reverse` for mobile. The info \"1/26 -- NEXT PROJECT\" now has options: above the title, below, or keep it in the top right. I\'ve chosen to keep the top right info, for now.\n\n\n**Adjusting Mobile Header**\n\nI\'ve decided to keep the \"NEXT PROJECT\" info in the top right, but adjusted its mobile display. Now, on smaller screens, it will be `flex justify-between w-full` when at the very top. I\'ve restructured the top row to accommodate this change in the app.\n\n\n**Revising Table Positioning**\n\nI\'m now revising the table\'s positioning, currently using `absolute` positioning, which is problematic on smaller screens. The screen must be tall and wide enough for the table to not overflow the window. I\'m updating the table\'s wrapper with relative positioning so I can more easily change the layout for small screens. I\'m now testing the relative positioning with the current large-screen layout.\n\n\n"
